 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "There's water all over the kitchen floor coming from under my Beko washing machine and its gone under the units to where the plug is, the machine is still going. What do I do?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Water near the plug or socket is dangerous. Please don't touch the plug, socket or machine if they or your hands are wet. If you can do it safely, switch off the circuit at the consumer unit (fuse box), keep away from the water, and don't use the machine again until a qualified electrician or appliance engineer has checked it. Do not touch any plugs or switches if water has reached them, as this poses a serious electric shock risk. From what you have described, the most likely cause is sump / tub-to-pump hose. The pump filter cap at the bottom front is a common leak, especially if it's been opened recently. Check it's screwed in fully and straight, and that its rubber seal is clean. Was it loose or not seated, tight and dry already, or is the cap or seal cracked or damaged?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "The pump filter cap is tight and the area behind the flap is dry. I'm standing in socks on the wet floor right now and the water's run back towards the socket. Should I get to the fuse box first?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, you should turn off the power at the mains immediately because water near a plug and socket is an electric shock hazard. Do not touch the machine or the floor while it is still running. From what you have checked, this needs an appliance engineer to look at it: the next steps are beyond the checks that are safe to do yourself."
  }
 ],
